[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Roughly 13,000 differential runs were executed: the ~130 hand-enumerated cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in `tests/differential.rs`, plus ad-hoc fuzzing over random float spellings,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hex-float spellings and float rounding boundaries. No case required a change to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/main.rs`, and nothing in `c_src/` was touched.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because there is nothing to record as "found and fixed", the rest of this file
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m documents the behaviours that *would* have produced a mismatch had the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation got them wrong, and how each was confirmed. These are the places a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m future change is most likely to break.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Control-flow paths in the C, and the input that reaches each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` is a fixed sequence: `good()` (which is `goodG2B()` then `goodB2G()`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then `bad()`. `stdin` is read exactly twice — once by `goodB2G`, once by `bad`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C path | Reached by |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `printLine` with a non-NULL pointer | every run (NULL is unreachable; all call sites pass literals) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `goodG2B` — constant `data = 2.0F` | every run, prints `50` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `goodB2G` `fgets` succeeds | any non-empty stdin |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `goodB2G` `fgets` returns NULL | empty stdin, `/dev/null`, closed fd, fd on a directory |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `goodB2G` `fabs(data) > 0.000001` true | `2e-6`, `7`, `1.0000001e-06` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `goodB2G` guard false → "This would result in a divide by zero" | `0`, `-0`, `abc`, `1e-06`, `nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `bad` `fgets` succeeds | ≥ 2 lines, or a line longer than 19 bytes |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `bad` `fgets` returns NULL → "fgets() failed." | 1 line or less on stdin |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `bad` division by zero, no guard | `0`, `-0`, `abc`, empty stdin |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `(int)` cast out of range | any divisor whose reciprocal ×100 exceeds 2^31 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours verified as byte-identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`(int)` of an out-of-range / infinite / NaN double.** In C this is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    undefined behaviour; gcc on x86-64 emits `cvttsd2si`, which returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    "integer indefinite" value `0x80000000`. Division by zero therefore prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `-2147483648`, not a trap and not `2147483647`. Rust's `as` cast *saturates*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    instead, so a naive `v as i32` would print `2147483647` for `+inf` and `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    for NaN. `c_double_to_int` reproduces the hardware behaviour. Confirmed at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the boundary with divisors `4.6566127e-08` / `4.6566129e-08` (either side of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `100.0 / 2^31`) and with `nan`, `inf`, `0`, `-0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`fgets` does not read past its newline, and stops at 19 bytes.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `CHAR_ARRAY_SIZE` is 20, so at most 19 bytes are stored. A line of 19 or more
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    payload bytes is *split*: `goodB2G` gets the first 19 bytes and `bad` gets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the remainder of the same line. Using a line-oriented read, or `read_line`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    would consume the whole line and change what `bad()` sees. Confirmed with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    18-, 19-, 20-, 23- and 25-byte lines, including one where the split lands
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    inside an exponent (`1.000000000000000e-40`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`fgets` returns NULL only when nothing was stored.** A final line without a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    trailing newline is still returned successfully; NULL comes from EOF-with-no-
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    bytes or from a read error. Both were exercised: EOF via empty stdin and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `/dev/null`, and a genuine read error via an fd opened on a directory
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`EISDIR`) — in that case the C prints `fgets() failed.` twice and the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    must too.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **`atof` is `strtod(s, NULL)`: longest-prefix parsing, `0.0` on failure.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Trailing junk is ignored rather than being an error (`5.` → 5.0,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `1,5` → 1.0, `01234567890123456789ZZZ` → truncated digits), a malformed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    exponent is not consumed (`1e` → 1.0), and `inf` / `infinity` / `nan` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `nan(chars)` and hex floats (`0X1.8p3`, `0x1p-149`) are all accepted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    case-insensitively. `0x` with no hex digit converts only the leading `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust's `str::parse::<f64>` accepts none of these forms, so a direct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `parse().unwrap_or(0.0)` would diverge on most of them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`atof` sees a C string.** `fgets` may store NUL bytes; the conversion stops
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    at the first one. Confirmed with `5\0abc`, `\0 5` and a flood of NULs from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `/dev/zero`. Input is also not required to be UTF-8, so the buffer is handled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    as bytes throughout (`\xff\xfe`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **`(float)` narrowing before the division.** `data` is a `float`, and it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the narrowed value that is divided and compared. This matters at the guard:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `1e-06` narrows to `9.99999997e-07`, which is *not* `> 0.000001`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `goodB2G` prints the divide-by-zero message while `bad()` prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `100000000` for the very same text. Values below `1e-46` narrow to `0.0f` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    values above `3.4028235e38` narrow to `inf`, both of which change the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    printed result. Doing the arithmetic in `f64` throughout would silently pass
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the happy-path tests and fail here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **`100.0 / data` is a double division.** The literal is a `double`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `data` is promoted; the result is then truncated to `int`. Computing in `f32`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    would round differently for divisors such as `3` or `1e-7`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Output formatting.** `printf("%s\n")` and `printf("%d\n")` — no extra
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    spacing, no padding, a trailing newline on every line, and nothing on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    stderr. The exit status is always 0, including on the `fgets() failed.`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    paths, so a test that only diffed stdout would not notice a translation that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    exited 1 on read failure.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **`argc`/`argv` are ignored.** Passing arguments changes nothing in either
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Note on unused files
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/cio.rs` and `translation/src/catof.rs` are not declared as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m modules by `main.rs` and are therefore not compiled; the shipped binary's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour comes entirely from `main.rs`. They were left untouched because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m removing or wiring them in would change nothing that is observable.
